#!/usr/bin/python3 -IB
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""Set up the libfprint synaptics0078 driver (Synaptics 06cb:0078 reader) without Windows.

The driver reads two kinds of files from /var/lib/fprint/synaptics0078 each time
fprintd opens the reader:

  module.bin              10500 bytes, the vendor's sensor module (sha256-pinned)
  program-<serial>.bin    18869 bytes, first byte 0x02: the capture program built
                          from this unit's calibration; <serial> is 12 lowercase hex

Commands (research/steps/b5-setup-design.md, "Proposed command line"):

  install                 whatever is missing: the vendor files (from the cache in
                          /var/lib/synaptics0078/vendor, else downloaded), the
                          calibration of this reader, the two data files. A valid
                          module plus a valid program for this reader is "already
                          set up": no download, no calibration.
  install --from-file F   the vendor files from F instead (cab, zip, setup exe, MSI,
                          DLL, bare module, bare template; repeatable)
  install --from DIR      phase A: copy files made on Windows (unchanged, below)
  recalibrate             calibrate again; the previous program is kept under
                          /var/lib/synaptics0078/backup/
  fetch [--from-file F]   the vendor files into the cache only; no reader needed
  status                  module, programs (matched against the reader in sysfs),
                          cache, last calibration, the source URLs; exit 0 ready, 1 not
  common options          --dry-run (change nothing; no root needed), --yes (no Enter
                          prompt: a 3-second countdown instead)

It runs as root. Python is started with `-IB` (no PYTHONPATH, no user site, no script
or current directory on the import path, no bytecode written). The tool itself uses
the standard library only; the shared code (synaptics0078: pins, template, vendor
extraction, the guarded USB session) is loaded from /usr/lib/synaptics0078 only after
every directory from / down to the package, and every file in it, is checked to be a
real directory or regular file owned by root and not writable by group or others. It
is loaded by its file location, not through sys.path. pyusb is imported only when a
real USB session opens.

Vendor files are untrusted. Downloads use curl with an exact argv (https only, size
and time caps, `-q` first so no curlrc is read) into a private temporary file in the
root-only cache directory, then are read once with a cap. An archive whose sha256 is
one of the known archives (synaptics0078.pins.ARCHIVES) is parsed in this process;
anything else (a re-signed cab, a --from-file that is not a bare module or template)
is parsed in a child started as nobody:

  setpriv --reuid=65534 --regid=65534 --clear-groups --no-new-privs \\
      prlimit --as=1073741824 --cpu=120 --nproc=64 --fsize=0 -- \\
      /usr/bin/python3 -IB -c "<load the checked package; vendor.child_entry()>"

with the archive on stdin, in a session of its own (no controlling terminal); its
framed output is read with a size cap and a timeout, and every pin is applied again here. Nothing downloaded is executed; no archive member
name becomes a path. The module pin and the template pin are the gates.

Calibration (design decision 9): the reader is found in sysfs before any download;
fprintd is stopped if it is active (it restarts on demand); the interface is claimed
(on busy: stop fprintd once more, wait 1 s, retry once); fingers off; the calibration
sequence of synaptics0078.session runs with its command guard and the pins; a check
score above 200 or a baseline out of range asks to lift the fingers and retry (3 tries).
The serial comes from RomInfo, as the driver derives it, and must equal the sysfs USB
serial. No module loaded (RomInfo byte 17 == 0): the sequence uploads it; any module
other than ours loaded: refused ("restart the computer").

The log is /var/log/synaptics0078-setup.log (root, 0600), with the serial replaced by
<serial>; the terminal gets the short steps only. calibration.json beside the backups
(/var/lib/synaptics0078) records the date, the check score, the baseline statistics,
the tool version and the template pin, never the serial.

Phase A source (install --from DIR): a directory the user prepared with
tools/synaptics0078_data.py from the fingerprint-06cb-0078 project (it needs the
Windows driver files). The source files are opened without following symlinks, must
be regular files of the exact size, are read once, checked in memory, and those same
bytes are written. Run through sudo (SUDO_UID set), the source directory and every data
file in it, like every --from-file, must belong to the user who ran sudo: anything else
is refused before it is listed or read, so the tool cannot be used to look into another
user's or root's files. Run from a real root shell (no SUDO_UID) the owner is not
checked, and the tool says so.

Test hooks (never set them on a real system; honoured only when not running as
root, and as root the tool refuses to run while any of them is set):
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_ROOT           prefix for every system path; no root
                                          needed, no chown, the owner checked is
                                          the test user. The package is then loaded
                                          from the checkout this script is in
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_MODULE_SHA256  the module.bin sha256 to accept
                                          (synthetic test data); without a pins file
                                          the programs' content is then not checked
                                          against the template (phase A test data)
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_EUID           the euid the root guard above sees
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_PINS           a pins file (synaptics0078.pins.Pins.to_json)
                                          for synthetic fixtures; also given to the
                                          unprivileged extractor
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_ARCHIVES       a JSON list of archives (the fields of
                                          pins.Archive) replacing pins.ARCHIVES
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_LIBDIR         the directory holding the synaptics0078
                                          package (default: see TEST_ROOT)
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_CURL           the curl to run (default under a test
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_SYSTEMCTL      root: the test root's usr/bin/<name>,
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_SETPRIV        which normally does not exist)
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_PRLIMIT
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_SYSFS          the directory standing for /sys (default
                                          under a test root: its sys/)
  SYNAPTICS0078_SETUP_TEST_TRANSPORT      a Python file defining make_transport(log,
                                          guard), returning a fake USB transport (the
                                          interface of synaptics0078.session.UsbTransport);
                                          under a test root without it nothing calibrates
"""

